# 076.RecAFilament -- protein and DNA in one system

Four RecA protomers of a nucleoprotein filament with the dT13 strand they
wrap, all-atom, under the full bonded model.

    make prep     build reca.nc
    make point    the energy table below
    make run      dynamics, drivable from VMD or UnityMol


## What it is for

074 and 075 exercise nucleic acids alone; 070-073 exercise proteins alone.
This is the only example where BOTH parameter sets are loaded at once, and
that is the thing it tests.

pdb2spn takes one --grp and one --nonbondedinteraction, so the Makefile
concatenates them. That works only because the type names cannot collide:
amber.grp prefixes every protein type with an UPPERCASE residue letter (ACA
for ALA's CA) while amber.dna.grp uses lowercase and digits (dC5, 1C1).
amber.dna.grp was built that way deliberately, for this case.

Checked rather than assumed, on the union the Makefile actually builds: 417
types used, 0 name shared between the two halves, 0 orphan (every type a
.grp names exists in the .nbi.ff), 0 (residue,atom) key mapped twice, and
the two .rbody files' residue sets disjoint. Note that amber.dna.grp and
amber.rna.grp do NOT concatenate with each other -- they share 49 type
names with different parameters, 1C1 being deoxyribose's C1' in one and
ribose's in the other. A DNA/RNA hybrid would need one of them regenerated
under a third prefix.


## Point energy, against AMBER on the same conformation

    term        BioSpring     AMBER        difference
    stretch     28329.64      28330.09       -0.45 kJ/mol  (-0.002 %)
    bend        40676.93      40206.46     +470.47 kJ/mol  (+1.17 %)
    dihedral    72728.78      68136.13    +4592.65 kJ/mol  (+6.74 %)

All in kJ/mol, at the input conformation, over 20088 stretching, 36732
bending and 51360 dihedral springs (123967 ghost particles).

AMBER's column excludes the terms whose atoms all sit inside a saturated
ring -- 245 bonds, 245 angles and 297 torsions, worth 334.15, 676.60 and
195.97 kJ/mol. --rigidbody freezes those rings and BioSpring emits no
bonded spring inside them, so charging it for terms it deliberately does
not model would be measuring the wrong thing (see 074's README for why the
rings are frozen).

Stretch agreeing to 0.45 kJ/mol out of 28330 is the expected result: a
harmonic bond retuned in place IS AMBER's term. Bend at +1.17 % is the
known ghost-particle residual. The dihedral's +6.7 % is the ghost rings'
idealised-geometry limit, and it is spread over the protein rather than
localised on the DNA: 3.5 kJ/mol per residue across all 1309, where the 13
nucleotides are 1 % of the system.


## Do NOT quench this model: the dihedral rings deform valence geometry

Left to quench (make run, viscosity without a random force), 6 ps gives:

    step        stretch     bend      dihedral    kinetic
    0           28329.64  40676.93   72728.78          --
    6 ps         9867.15  13691.26  -15281.81       12.79

All kJ/mol. Nothing diverges -- but the negative dihedral is a real defect,
not a convention, and it is worth knowing before trusting a minimised
structure from this model.

AMBER's torsion energy is a sum of k(1 + cos(n.phi - gamma)) with k > 0, so
it is >= 0 by construction. A ring that reproduced its term faithfully
would report >= 0 too. Below zero means the ring is no longer tracking the
torsion it was calibrated for.

What it tracks instead is bond length. Scale a structure uniformly and
every angle and dihedral is preserved exactly, so AMBER's torsion energy
cannot move -- measured on ubiquitin, it does not, staying at 3050.12
kJ/mol to the digit. BioSpring's goes 3009.46 at scale 1.00, 9028.09 at
0.94 and 12352.49 at 1.06. BEND is unaffected (262 kJ/mol throughout): this
is the dihedral construction alone.

And it is a FORCE artifact, not just a reported one. Quenching ubiquitin
with the dihedral families off reaches stretch 1.39 and bend 96.54 kJ/mol;
with them on, the same quench stops at 636.54 and 905.62. The rings buy
their own relaxation by paying about 1500 kJ/mol of valence strain.

Cause, measured rather than argued: ring radius and plane separation used
to be scaled together by a constant (RING_SCALE = 4.0), which leaves the
harmonic content invariant while shrinking exactly this bond-length
sensitivity as 1/scale^2. Placing each ghost as the rotated image of a REAL
atom (49ffc1e, which is what made the Jacobians unnecessary) fixed the
radius to that atom's own -- ghost radii fell from a median of 3.10 A to
1.33 A -- and left the constant with nothing to act on, so it was dropped
as dead code shortly after.

Both versions were built and run through the same scaling test. Over the
same +/-6 %, the pre-49ffc1e model's dihedral energy spans 623.75 kJ/mol
and drifts monotonically; the current one spans 9343.03 and is a sharp V
about the native scale. That is a measured 15x loss of scale insensitivity,
which is what the constant was there to buy.

Worth stating plainly: the trade was real in both directions. Placing
ghosts by rotation is what removed the Jacobians, and the torque and
per-axis energy validations that followed it both passed -- they rotate
rigidly or evaluate one conformation, so neither can vary a bond length,
and neither could have caught this.

Where this matters, stated carefully. The point energy above is measured on
a conformation OpenMM prepared, and there it is 1.3 % from AMBER on
ubiquitin (3009.46 against 3050.12). That says the reported energy is right
for a structure handed to the model; it says nothing about the structure
the model itself settles into.

And there is no safe regime to retreat to: BioSpring has no thermostat. Its
dynamics is viscosity-damped Newtonian motion with no random force -- a
quench, as model.msp says in its own comment -- and the only temperature in
the code, rigidbody.montecarlo_temperature, belongs to the rigid-body Monte
Carlo instead. Every run therefore relaxes toward exactly the state that
lets this artifact express itself fully.

So the honest summary is neither "ignore it" nor "the model is broken". The
model runs, keeps its fold, and reproduces AMBER's per-axis torques and its
point energy. What is biased is the valence geometry it converges to: 636
kJ/mol of bond strain where the same quench without the dihedral families
reaches 1.39.


## The system, and what was done to it

3CMX is the RecA-DNA filament this example's system comes from -- the same
structure the coarse-grained example 022.RecA uses, at all-atom resolution
here, which makes this a different model of the same object rather than a
reproduction of it.

Kept: the four RecA protomers whose backbone is continuous in the crystal
(chain A residues 37-333, 1001-1333, 2001-2333, 3001-3333, split into four
chains here since they are four molecules) and the dT13 strand. 10020 heavy
atoms in, about 20000 once hydrogenated.

Dropped, and why:

  * ADP, Mg and AlF4. No AMBER template, and no parameters of ours either --
    modelling them would be a separate piece of work, not a preparation
    step.
  * the fifth protomer. Residues 4155-4167 and 4197-4204 are disordered and
    absent from the crystal, and rebuilding 21 residues of backbone is a
    modelling decision. It is also the protomer that touches the DNA least
    -- 153 atom pairs within 5 A, against 264 to 373 for the other four
    (measured, not assumed).
  * the strand's free 5'-monophosphate, leaving a 5'-OH. AMBER has no
    template for it and it is what tleap builds from a sequence.

Added: the terminal OXT of each protein chain, by pdbfixer. It reports no
missing side-chain atom anywhere, which is worth knowing -- a missing heavy
atom is not a warning but a template error deep inside createSystem.

Then minimised and run 5 ps at 300 K with GBn2 implicit solvent, like every
other structure in these examples. A crystal is cold, packed and away from
the force field's own equilibrium; it is never used as deposited.

reca_dna_raw.pdb is what went INTO that preparation -- the filtered crystal,
before hydrogens. It is kept because the preparation is expensive (hours of
CPU at this size) and without its input it cannot be reproduced. Use
reca_dna.pdb for everything else; that is what the Makefile builds from.


## Stiffness, deliberately 250 and not 3000

074.DNADuplex runs at 3000 kJ/mol/A^2 because the furanose rings carry no
bonded rule of their own and are held by the mesh alone. Here the value is
250, the protein examples' validated one, so those rings relax more than
they do in 074.

That is the right trade for this example: raising the stiffness for 13
nucleotides would stiffen 1188 protein residues with them, and the pucker
is already measured properly in 074 and 075. What this example is about is
the two chemistries coexisting.
